{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And by these you shall become impure</b> — By these that are destined to be mentioned below in the matter, you shall become impure (Rashi here; Sifra). Here Scripture teaches you that the carcass of an impure animal imparts impurity, and at the end of the section (Leviticus 11:39) it explained regarding a pure animal. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 11:26) (at verse 26). If so, why did Scripture divide them and why did it specify them? It should have said: \"Whoever touches the carcass of any animal shall be impure until evening, and whoever carries of its carcass shall wash his garments and be impure until evening.\" The correct explanation is that here it mentioned \"בְּנִבְלָתָם\" (in their carcass) without specification and did not mention death concerning them, to teach that slaughter has no effect on them — whoever touches them when they are not alive becomes impure. But there it said \"וְכִי יָמוּת מִן הַבְּהֵמָה\" (when an animal dies), to say that if it was slaughtered it does not impart impurity. The sense of the section: it mentioned \"וּלְאֵלֶּה תִּטַּמָּאוּ\" to say that among those mentioned regarding fish, birds, and swarming creatures there is not this impurity, but only regarding those which it will mention — namely, the animal that does not split the hoof and does not chew the cud. And it said (at verse 26) \"they are impure to you; whoever touches them shall become impure\" — with the impurity mentioned above. And it returned and said (at verses 27–28) that whatever walks on its paws, among beasts as well, imparts impurity — one who touches [it] shall be impure until evening, and one who carries [it] shall additionally wash his garments. And likewise \"and this is what is impure to you among swarming creatures\" (Leviticus 11:29) — concerning what it is about to mention. Rabbi Abraham erred here. Now, above, in the matter of the dietary prohibition, it mentioned the animal that does not split the hoof and does not chew the cud, but did not mention that which walks on its paws among beasts. Perhaps because it is not customary to eat them, it did not specify them, but left them within the general rule it stated (Leviticus 11:3), that we may eat the animal that has a divided hoof, splits the hoof, and chews the cud, and no other — and חַיָּה (beast) is included in בְּהֵמָה (animal), as it says: \"These are the animals you may eat: the gazelle, the deer, and the roebuck\" (Deuteronomy 14:4–5). For our Sages in Torat Kohanim, throughout the entire section, there are midrashic interpretations. \"For all the animals — they are impure to you\" (Leviticus 11:26) — [this refers to] a limb from a living animal (Sifra, parashah 4:2). \"And whatever walks on its paws\" — [this refers to] a limb from a dead animal (chapter 6:6). For Scripture lengthens and mentions concerning them many impurities, to hint that all of them and part of them impart impurity — both through the death of the entire body and through the death of a limb from it when it is detached from the living body." "The reason for <b>he shall be impure until evening</b> throughout the section is that in the evening he becomes pure if he washes his flesh in water. But it abbreviated regarding the washing because it mentioned (Leviticus 11:25) the washing of the garments of the one who carries, which is immersion — all the more so must he wash his body. And when it finished mentioning all the impurities of carcasses and swarming creatures, it mentioned immersion in vessels: \"it must be put in water and be impure until evening and become pure\" (Leviticus 11:32), and the same law applies to a person, for it is derived from it. And in another section (Leviticus 17:15) it explained: \"Any person who eats a carcass or a torn animal, whether native-born or proselyte, shall wash his garments and bathe in water and be impure until evening,\" and it also mentioned the punishment: \"and if he does not wash [his garments] and does not bathe his flesh, he shall bear his iniquity\" (ibid. verse 16). This is a general principle for all impurity of carcasses, and from it the law of swarming creatures is derived. And the washing was already mentioned here regarding swarming creatures, as I have written. And Scripture was stricter regarding carrying than regarding touching, in [requiring] the washing of garments, because the way of those who touch is to touch with the hand gently, but one who carries them by way of a load — it weighs upon him, and his garments will assist him in most cases."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The phrase 'ונשתבש רבי אברהם בכאן' (Rabbi Abraham erred here) refers to Ibn Ezra; Ramban consistently uses this name for him."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:11.749558655Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}